# Learning Objectives
Insert key-value pairs: 
Added new records using unique keys to model fast keyed storage in a hash-based structure.

Retrieve values efficiently: 
Looked up existing keys directly and used safe lookup checks or .get() so missing keys did not cause unexpected crashes.

Update existing values: 
Modified values for existing keys in place (such as updating stock counts or order statuses) rather than creating duplicate entries.

Remove entries: 
Deleted existing records cleanly and tested removal behavior on missing or empty entries.

Understand hashing concepts:
Connected key hashing, slot/bucket indexing, collision handling, and load factor behavior to real-world lookup speed.

Requirements
Create and populate a dictionary: 
Built and populated a Python dictionary representing a real-world scenario with meaningful keys and values.

Demonstrate lookup operations: 
Tested retrieving values for valid keys as well as keys that were not present.

Demonstrate update operations: 
Updated existing key-value pairs and verified how duplicate-key updates overwrote or merged values.

Demonstrate delete operations: 
Removed keys from the dictionary and handled attempts to delete absent keys safely.

Test edge cases: 
Checked empty dictionaries, non-existent keys, empty/boundary inputs, and repeated operations during execution.

Create a real-world scenario:
Modeled a practical application (such as an online inventory or order lookup system) to demonstrate how hash tables work in practice.

Discussion Board Reflection
Completing this assignment deepened my understanding of how Python dictionaries apply 
hash table principles to perform fast key-value insertions, lookups, updates, and deletions. 
Designing a realistic scenario and testing edge cases—such as empty dictionaries, missing keys,
and overwriting existing entries—helped clarify how defensive checks keep programs reliable. 
One challenge was ensuring that edge-case handling and documentation stayed clear and consistent
across both the code comments and README.md; I overcame this by running targeted test cases for 
invalid or missing lookups and writing past-tense notes documenting what I implemented and 
verified. In general, a hash table converts a key via a hash function to locate storage slots
quickly, avoiding full-list scans and improving lookup efficiency. Collisions occur when 
different keys hash to the same slot, which can slow operations if slots fill up or cluster. 
By using well-distributed keys, safe lookup/deletion methods, and resizing when needed, hash 
tables help applications stay fast and responsive as data grows.
